From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Date: Tue, 28 Jun 1994 09:04:39 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-179] Evolution of Time Preference

>From Robin Hansen:
> Alan Rogers, "The Evolution of Time Preference by Natural Selection",
>   American Economic Review, June 1994, V84 N3, pp.460-481.
...
>1) An asexually-reproducing creature should discount the future at
>   exactly the rate of population growth.  If population is growing at
>   2% per year, and an asexual creature can get more than that for
>   sure in a long-term investment to be paid to its descendants, and if
>   certain amount of wealth is equally useful in making babies at all
>   times, then its genes will do better if it invests.
...
>A) If you think of yourself as an asexual reproducer, planning to live a
>   very long time yourself, planning on copying yourself lots as an
>   upload, or planning on reproducing via your memes rather than your genes,
>   then you may want to save more than folks following their evolution-given
>   time preferences.

This seems to me to assume that 100% of the parent is passed on to the
child.  Memetic reproduction generally involves the passing on of only a
small percentage of the parent's memes, suggesting that someone reproducing
_only_ memetically would discount the future even more heavily than a
sexual reproducer.  The only examples I can think of of people who
reproduce only memetically on purpose are priests and Shakers (?), to whom
this discounting apparently does not apply.  Are there any more typical
examples that bear it out, or that contradict it?

Kennita


