From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Tue, 28 Jun 1994 12:08:40 -0400
Subject: [#94-6-180] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

 I disagree with the claim that there is or will be a trend towards
increasing uncertainty in the development of military hardware. While
it is likely that change in this area will accelerate (although I see
no signs that this is happening yet), the improvements in information
gathering abilities have substantially reduced uncertainties over the
past few decades, and all indications are that this trend will continue
to offset the problems associated with faster hardware development. For
instance, there was a big debate in the late 50's about a missile gap
between the US and the USSR. That largely vanished about 1960 when spy
planes and satellites became good enough to count missiles reliably. Or
compare the years that it took for the genocide Stalin forced on the
Ukraine to be acknowledged in the west, versus the speed with which
faxes about the Tiannamen Square massacre got out.
 But what about non-hardware weapons such as viruses that are primarily
information? Since they are much harder to observe and easy to hide via
encryption, they have a potential for increasing uncertainties. But it
is hard to imagine that they will provide much motivation for a first
strike, because it is easy to keep backup copies on other continents or
in other orbits, making geographically based attacks ineffective at anything
other than killing off the developers. And if the developers are AI's rather
than humans, even that is likely to be pointless.

 The things I fear will motivate wars are contention over resources and
a sharp separation between biological and digital intelligence. The
desire to acquire more resources has been an important cause of wars in
the past, and accelerating economic growth is likely to increase the value
of most physical resources.
 The ability to divide the world into "us" and "them aliens" is an important
part of making war acceptable, and improved worldwide communications
(especially internet) are rapidly making it unnatural for people to
organize their choices of friends and enemies on the kind of geographical
basis that has been needed for war so far. If AI is achieved in a way that
produces very non-human thought patterns, it may be easy for humans and AI's
to hate each other. This is one of the reasons that I don't like the classical
symbol-processing approach to AI, and hope that the initial AI's are uploaded
humans, or if that is impractical then a connectionist approach that closely
mimics the way human intelligence evolved.

-----------------------------------------------------------
