From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 28 Jun 1994 12:02:51 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-184] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

Gregory Sullivan clearly cars alot about this subject, since he offers
us a 1700 word essay on the topic.  But I found his discussion, as
I've found most discussion on this topic, to be far too quick to jump
to conclusions.  For example, I simply can't accept bald claims like
the following without much more careful supporting arguments: 

>1) When nanotech is developed the design space for weapons will open up
>dramatically.  

Did computers dramatically expand the weapon design space?  Was this
destabilizing?

>2) The design space will be changing quickly and unpredictably as new
>advancements are made in science and technology and as new capabilities are
>realized.

Why is nanotech more unpredictable that previous technology?

>3) The time scale for the development and deployment of weapons will be
>dramatically compressed. 

Compressed relative to what?  Sure, general economic progress seems to
be accelerating, which encourages shorter development times, but why
should nanotech be much different from the trend?  Why should
development times decrease lots relative to the other relevant
political and economic time scales?

>4) Warring factions may suffer from radical uncertainty about the nature of
>opposition weaponry. 

Why should they be much more uncertain than today?  

5) In the current pre-nanotech environment warring parties can 
>[no longer] relatively accurately gauge the power of adversaries and 
>potential adversaries. 

Why should this be?  How is Drexler's scenario even remotely plausibe?
That is, how could an overwhelming mass of complex automated military
hardware possibly be designed, evolved, tested, constructed, and
integrated by a third rate military power in so short a time that
other powers are caught unprepared?

>6) As nanotech develops the balance between offensive and defensive war
>technology may swing wildly.  

Why? Why? Why?  I'll quit quoting here - I think I've made my point.
Instead going on with more such claims, you need to offer deeper
arguments and analysis in favor of fewer claims.  Saying nanotech is
different, and therefore uncertain, and therefore destabilizing is not
to me a sufficient analysis.

Robin Hanson

