The Crash of '94- Update Information by Phone and Internet 
by Amara Graps

The Great Comet Crash will soon absorb everyone's attention.
The lucky, hardy, and dedicated astronomers will be at the
big telescopes July 16-22, gathering data by the megabyte.
But if you're not a direct member of a research team observing
this comet, but are still excited by this event, how can you
get the latest information?

You can:

1) Call your research friends, 

2) Watch CNN or NASA Select,

3) Set up your telescope in your backyard and try to ignore (!)
the waxing gibbous moon and the city lights,

4) Call 
	617-497-4168	Sky & Telescope's Skyline Recording
	900-884-6722	Planetary Society's Impact Hotline,
	900-773-COMET	The Nature Company Hotline.

5) Subscribe to the "Comet/Jupiter Collision Mailing List." 
Send mail to listproc@seds.lpl.arizona.edu (no subject) with the 
message: SUBSCRIBE SL9 Firstname Lastname.

6) Get the "Frequently Asked Questions about the Collision of Comet
Shoemaker-Levy 9 with Jupiter".  Available via anonymous ftp to 
tamsun.tamu.edu (128.194.15.32) in the /pub/comet directory. 

7) Buy the Sky and Telescope's January and July 1994 
issues for a wonderful overview of this exciting event.

8) Travel through Internet using anonymous ftp and World Wide Web to
find more information.   
